{
  "_copyright": "Copyright (C) 2026 Safari — GPL-3.0-or-later",
  "_note": "The keyboard map this session ships, and the single source of it. iarch-keybinds renders .config/hypr/generated/keybinds.lua from this file plus the differences a person has saved in ~/.config/iarch/keybinds.json, so a bind is written here or in that file and nowhere else. Until Part 5 the direction was the other way round -- tools/extract_keybinds.py read this record out of .config/hypr/keybind.lua -- and the reversal is the point: a report about a file cannot be edited, because the next extract overwrites the edit, which is why the Shortcuts page was read-only. Every spelling a bind here may use is recorded in data/design/keybinds.json against the pinned compositor, and iarch-keybinds check validates this file against that record rather than against an assumption.",
  "version": 2,
  "migrated_from": {
    "version": 1,
    "by": "tools/migrate_keybinds_v2.py",
    "source": "the v1 extract of .config/hypr/keybind.lua, kept in git history",
    "bindings": 77,
    "rewritten": [
      "super + shift + ctrl + down",
      "super + shift + ctrl + left",
      "super + shift + ctrl + right",
      "super + shift + ctrl + up",
      "super + shift + down",
      "super + shift + left",
      "super + shift + right",
      "super + shift + up"
    ],
    "rewritten_note": "Written in the lower-case spelling the v1 record used for the key part of a combination -- it spelled modifiers upper-case and some keys lower-case, so `SUPER + SHIFT + right` and `SUPER + tab` sat side by side. A key name resolves case-insensitively (src/keybinds/Resolver.cpp:58), so both spellings bind the same key; the record normalises on one rather than carrying two.",
    "why_rewritten": "Each reached for a pre-Lua dispatcher name through `hyprctl dispatch`, which on a Lua config is evaluated as `hl.dispatch(resizeactive 30 0)` and is not Lua. The replacements and their argument tables are in data/design/keybinds.json.",
    "bound_here": [
      "SUPER + H"
    ],
    "why_bound_here": "SUPER + H, SUPER + SHIFT + H and SUPER + K were declared in the Lua and never bound, and v1 carried them with the status reserved so nothing would reassign them by accident. SUPER + H is the shortcut overlay this part exists to build, so it is bound; the other two stay reserved."
  },
